                        "review": "Moonchild is an old-school fantasy RPG developed and published by Aldorlea Games that proudly wears its inspirations on its sleeve. From the moment the adventure begins, it becomes clear that the game is designed for players who grew up with pixel-art classics and enjoy lengthy journeys filled with exploration, turn-based battles, and a steady stream of character progression. At the center of the story is Queen Calypso, whose life is turned upside down after her child is taken away under mysterious circumstances. Her quest to recover the Moonchild serves as the driving force behind an adventure that balances personal stakes with traditional fantasy elements, giving players a reason to remain invested throughout its substantial runtime.\n\nThe game's strongest asset is the sheer amount of content it provides. Moonchild features a cast of nine recruitable characters, each contributing unique abilities and combat roles that encourage experimentation. The party management system adds welcome flexibility, allowing players to adjust their lineup according to the challenges ahead. Battles follow a familiar turn-based structure, rewarding preparation and thoughtful use of spells, equipment, and summoned allies. Rather than relying on random encounters, enemies are visible on the map, a design decision that helps maintain pacing and grants players more control over their exploration. Combined with multiple difficulty options, the game succeeds in making itself accessible to both experienced RPG enthusiasts and those looking for a gentler introduction to the genre.\n\nExploration plays a significant role in the overall experience. Moonchild offers numerous side activities and hidden discoveries that reward players willing to stray from the main path. Dungeons are packed with treasure, optional encounters, and opportunities to strengthen the party, while the world itself feels expansive enough to justify repeated visits to earlier locations. The game's progression system consistently provides new abilities and equipment, ensuring that advancement feels meaningful from beginning to end. Its campaign easily stretches beyond twenty hours, and completionists can spend considerably more time uncovering everything it has to offer.\n\nVisually, Moonchild embraces a retro aesthetic that will immediately appeal to fans of classic console RPGs. The environments are bright and expressive, while the sprite work captures the charm of an earlier era without feeling overly simplistic. The soundtrack complements the adventure well, shifting comfortably between peaceful exploration themes and more dramatic compositions during key story moments. Although the presentation lacks the technical sophistication of modern releases, it achieves exactly what it sets out to do: create a nostalgic atmosphere that feels authentic rather than imitative.\n\nThat said, Moonchild is not without its limitations. Its commitment to traditional design occasionally results in uneven pacing, particularly during longer dungeon sequences that can begin to feel repetitive. The narrative, while engaging, rarely strays far from familiar fantasy territory, and some dialogue lacks the polish found in larger RPG productions. Players seeking innovative mechanics or a deeply layered storyline may find parts of the experience predictable. However, these shortcomings are easier to overlook when viewed through the lens of the game's intended audience. Moonchild is less concerned with reinventing the genre and more focused on celebrating it.\n\nIn the end, Moonchild succeeds because of its sincerity. It delivers a lengthy and enjoyable role-playing adventure built around exploration, party customization, and a heartfelt story about family and determination. Aldorlea Games demonstrates a clear understanding of what makes classic RPGs memorable, offering an experience that feels both nostalgic and approachable. While it may not stand alongside the genre's all-time greats, it remains a worthwhile adventure for anyone eager to revisit the style and spirit of retro fantasy gaming.\n\nRating: 6/10",
